WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.960
- Hey, LA, are you there?
- Hey, Nahre, I'm here.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.020
What's up?
- Hey.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.410
I wanted to ask you a question.

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:07.369
First, listen to this.

00:00:07.369 --> 00:00:10.369
(upbeat funk music)

00:00:11.948 --> 00:00:13.781
♪ Hey ♪

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.070
Sounds like a James
Brown song, doesn't it?

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.070
- Yeah.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:21.330
- But it's not, what is it about this

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.470
that we're both hearing that
makes us think of James Brown?

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.110
What's going on musically
that makes James Brown

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.763
sound like James Brown?
♪ One, two, three, four ♪

00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:31.882
("Get Up" by James Brown)

00:00:31.882 --> 00:00:33.450
♪ Get up, get on up ♪

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.370
Screams, spins, and splits all make

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.290
for a great show, but
what was the Minister

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.730
of the New, New Superheavy Funk doing?

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:42.660
To answer that question,
we have to go back to 1964

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.810
when funk was born.
(upbeat funk music)

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.260
- So a lot of people say that funk started

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.100
with Papa's Got A Brand New Bag in 1965.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.540
Others say it started
in 1967 with Cold Sweat,

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.670
but James Brown says in his autobiography

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:59.060
that his sound really
started to change in 1964

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.620
with his album Out of Sight.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:01.973
♪ Got to tie your sneakers on ♪

00:01:01.973 --> 00:01:04.557
"Out of Sight was another
beginning, musically

00:01:04.557 --> 00:01:06.497
"and professionally, you
can hear the band and me

00:01:06.497 --> 00:01:08.797
"start to move in a whole
other direction rhythmically.

00:01:08.797 --> 00:01:11.687
"The horns, the guitar,
the vocals, everything

00:01:11.687 --> 00:01:13.817
"was starting to be used
to establish all kind

00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:16.597
"of rhythms at once, I as
trying to get every aspect

00:01:16.597 --> 00:01:19.245
"of the production to contribute
to the rhythmic patterns."

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:22.963
♪ You know you're out of sight ♪

00:01:22.963 --> 00:01:23.796
♪ Ow ♪

00:01:23.796 --> 00:01:26.033
- Brown got away from melodies.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.950
His music was very rhythmically focused,

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.940
even the tambour, his delivery
style, was percussive.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.240
- Yeah.
- And I think

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.910
that's what made funk such a grooving,

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:40.940
such a popular sound
because even the musicians,

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.320
they are not really always singing.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.320
It's more group chanting.
- Yeah.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.860
- But it's not driven
by a series of chords.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:52.540
It's driven by rhythmic interactions.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.240
- And not only was he
using every instrument

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.160
to add to the song's rhythmic pattern,

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.110
but he was creating a whole new pattern.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.590
So most music of that
era gave heavy emphasis

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:04.610
to the back beat, on the two
and the four of the measure.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:05.910
It sounded like this.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:08.309
One, two, three, four.

00:02:08.309 --> 00:02:10.852
One, two, three, four.
(upbeat percussion music)

00:02:10.852 --> 00:02:13.320
One, two, three, four.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.280
What James Brown did was he
put the emphasis on the one,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.600
on the downbeat of the measure.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.277
And that sounds like
one, two, three, four.

00:02:23.225 --> 00:02:25.791
One, two, three, four.
(upbeat percussion music)

00:02:25.791 --> 00:02:30.278
♪ One, two, three, man, get funky ♪

00:02:30.278 --> 00:02:33.890
♪ Man, get funky, huh, man, get funky ♪

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.020
Emphasizing the one
created space in the groove

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:39.220
so that the band could add
syncopation to the other beat.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.710
Syncopation is just
placing rhythms or accents

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.290
in places where we don't expect it.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.430
So if these are the strong beats.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.920
Three, four, one, two, three, four.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.830
Syncopation is just putting accents

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:54.020
in between the strong beats.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:55.570
So sound like,

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.160
one and two and three and
four and (beat boxes).

00:03:02.773 --> 00:03:05.755
("Mother Popcorn Part 1" by James Brown)

00:03:05.755 --> 00:03:08.172
♪ Yeah, yeah ♪

00:03:09.571 --> 00:03:10.450
♪ Yeah ♪
- So here is some

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:12.400
of the observations that I've picked up

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.420
while listening to James Brown songs.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.970
The first thing is that
his voice is very unique.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:21.970
The quality of how he
sings is almost shouting

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.950
and singing, it's somewhere in between.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:28.100
And his melodies and
phrases are more like staffs

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:31.460
rather than long, lyrical melodies.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.850
- This is James Brown, and
when doing Sound Field, y'all,

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:36.000
make it funky (laughs).

00:03:37.313 --> 00:03:39.957
I do what I can to mimic him

00:03:39.957 --> 00:03:43.260
and to give it a real heavier rasp.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.240
So I'll growl a little bit,
you know what I'm saying?

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.400
- Okay.
- I just wanna do my thing.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.550
You know, movin', groovin',
like a love machine.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:52.383
Is that alright?

00:03:52.383 --> 00:03:53.615
- Yes.
- Yes, sir.

00:03:53.615 --> 00:03:55.530
- (laughs) Indeed.
- James would take

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.960
very simplistic approaches.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.570
He would take a lyric and give it rhythm,

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.580
and then, he would wrap it with his soul

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:03.980
and dip it in funk (laughs).

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:07.790
That was preachin', you
know, when they would preach,

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:08.905
they would (grunts).

00:04:08.905 --> 00:04:11.070
It's called hoopin'.
- It's all

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:16.060
from the black church, his
influence was more the preachers

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.730
than the singers per se.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.980
His phrasing, and then, again (grunts),

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.410
you know, at the end.
- Yep.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:26.830
- And the preachers, they
are performing in a style

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:29.590
that generates responses
from the congregation.

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.350
James Brown does the
very same thing on stage.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:37.240
He is catering to the
needs of his audience,

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:41.440
and they understood, they
identified with his performance.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.300
And that's why he remains so popular.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.760
At the same time, it is also the reason

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.140
why he did not top really the pop charts.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:51.816
He was too much, but
it was just too black.

00:04:51.816 --> 00:04:53.840
